# SQL's `<>`: DS0107, with a fix to `!=`.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | where customerId <> "c-001"
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..48
  PIPELINE@0..47
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    WHERE_OP@20..47
      IDENT@20..25 "where"
      WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
      BIN_EXPR@26..47
        NAME_REF@26..36
          IDENT@26..36 "customerId"
        WHITESPACE@36..37 " "
        LTGT@37..39 "<>"
        WHITESPACE@39..40 " "
        LITERAL@40..47
          STRING@40..47 "\"c-001\""
  WHITESPACE@47..48 "\n"

error[DS0107]: `<>` is not an operator
  --> query:1:38
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | where customerId <> "c-001"
  |                                      ^^ this is SQL's "not equal"
  |
  = help: use `!=` to test that values differ: `customerId != "c-001"`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from 'orders.csv' | where customerId != "c-001"
which parses without errors
